# Rules to be followed for UI Development Projects  
## Core Principles  
We follow these UI development principles:  
Use **Tailwind CSS utility classes** for all styling  
Use **Flowbite components** where applicable  
Prioritize **responsive design** from the start (mobile-first)  
Ensure full accessibility (a11y) compliance  
Prefer **semantic HTML** over div-heavy structures  
Use **flexbox** for 1D layouts and **grid** for 2D layouts  
Avoid custom CSS unless absolutely necessary  
Skip inline styles; use Tailwind classes consistently  
Stick to a design system using Tailwind and Flowbite utilities

## Layout and Structure  
For layout systems, follow these guidelines:  
Use flex, flex-col, flex-row, justify-*, items-* for linear layouts  
Use grid, grid-cols-*, gap-*, and place-items-* for grid-based layouts  
Use container, mx-auto, and max-w-* for page structure and centering  
Use w-full, min-w-*, max-w-*, h-screen, min-h-* for sizing  
Avoid absolute and fixed unless for overlays, tooltips, or modals  
Prefer relative positioning for child layout containment

## Responsive Design and Media Queries  
For responsive behavior:  
Use Tailwind's responsive prefixes (sm:, md:, lg:, xl:, 2xl:)  
Design mobile-first, and scale up progressively  
Use hidden, block, flex, grid with breakpoints for conditional rendering  
Avoid writing custom media queries – Tailwind utilities should cover most use cases

## Tailwind Usage  
When using Tailwind:  
Use Tailwind's spacing scale (p-4, m-2, gap-6) instead of raw pixel values  
Use text-*, font-*, leading-*, tracking-* for typography  
Use rounded-*, shadow-*, border-*, ring-* for UI depth and borders  
Prefer bg-*, text-*, and hover:* classes over custom CSS  
Use transition, duration-*, ease-* for animation  
Use dark: classes for dark mode theming when required  
Avoid overriding Tailwind globally or creating new utility classes unnecessarily

## Flowbite Integration  
When using Flowbite:  
Use Flowbite components like buttons, dropdowns, modals, and tabs  
Ensure required JavaScript initialization is done for interactive components  
Customize Flowbite components using Tailwind classes, not custom CSS  
Follow Flowbite documentation for accessibility and component setup  
Avoid mixing other UI libraries with Flowbite to maintain consistency

## Accessibility and Semantics  
Ensure all components are:  
Built with semantic HTML (<main>, <button>, <nav>, etc.)  
Have appropriate ARIA attributes (aria-*) where needed  
Keyboard-navigable  
Use focus:* and outline-* Tailwind classes for focus states  
Include proper alt text for images and ARIA labels for interactive elements

## Component and Code Organization  
When structuring UI code:  
Keep components small and reusable  
Co-locate related files (component + test + style)  
Use consistent naming: feature.component.ts, feature-card.tsx, etc.  
Extract repeated UI pieces into shared components  
Avoid tightly coupling logic and UI – delegate data and logic to hooks/services

## Performance and Optimization  
For optimized UI:  
Lazy-load heavy components  
Use loading="lazy" on images  
Use Tailwind’s utility-based classes for performance and caching  
Avoid rendering off-screen or unnecessary elements  
Use skeleton loaders or spinners for loading states  
Use aspect-ratio, object-cover for image responsiveness
## Theming and Design Tokens  
For consistency in design:  
Follow Tailwind’s color palette (text-primary, bg-gray-100, etc.)  
Customize via tailwind.config.js for tokens like spacing, colors, and fonts  
Use design tokens via Tailwind variables instead of hardcoded values  
Maintain light/dark themes using dark: variant support  
Avoid introducing random hex values; use configured theme palette

## Error and State Handling  
Ensure all UI states are handled:  
Use placeholders, skeletons, or fallback text for loading states  
Use error messages that are accessible and visually distinct  
Use Tailwind text-red-*, bg-red-* classes for errors  
Avoid using toast-only error handling — errors should appear inline where possible  
Use aria-live regions for dynamically changing content

## Forms and Controls  
For building forms:  
Use Tailwind for input styling (form-input, focus:outline-none, etc.)  
Use Flowbite form components where available  
Maintain spacing and alignment using flex, grid, or space-* classes  
Group inputs with labels using semantic HTML (<label for="">)  
Validate inputs visually and with screen reader accessibility

